# uping — Debian/Ubuntu package notes
#
# This package installs a system deployment: one binary in /usr/bin, a systemd
# unit, and a writable data directory under /var/lib. The `~/uping` checkout
# documented in the README keeps working and is unaffected; use one or the
# other for a given port.

## First start

The package deliberately ships **no** `/etc/uping/config.toml`: an existing
configuration is never overwritten, and there is no useful default to guess.
Create it from the packaged example:

    sudo install -d -o root -g uping -m 0750 /etc/uping
    sudo install -o root -g uping -m 0640 \
        /usr/share/uping/config.toml.example /etc/uping/config.toml
    sudo editor /etc/uping/config.toml

Then validate it and start the service:

    sudo -u uping /usr/bin/uping --check /etc/uping/config.toml
    sudo systemctl enable --now uping

`serve_http`/`https_listen` failures (for example a VPN address that is not up
yet) do not stop the process; check `journalctl -u uping` for warnings.

## Paths

    /usr/bin/uping                                  the binary
    /usr/lib/systemd/system/uping.service           the system unit
    /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/uping.conf                  creates /var/lib/uping
    /etc/uping/config.toml                          yours; the package never ships it
    /usr/share/uping/config.toml.example            template for /etc/uping/config.toml
    /var/lib/uping/                                 writable state, owner uping

The packaged example puts the local CA and the generated leaves under
`/var/lib/uping` because `/etc` is not writable by the service user:

    [internal]
    ca_dir   = "/var/lib/uping/internal/ca"
    cert_dir = "/var/lib/uping/internal"

An ACME deployment can keep `cert_dir = "/var/lib/uping/cert"` instead.

## Capability

The unit carries `AmbientCapabilities=CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE` and an empty
capability bounding set apart from it. That is why there is no `setcap` step in
this package: upgrading the binary does not need one.

## Upgrades and removal

* `/etc/uping/config.toml` is not part of the package, so an upgrade never
  touches it.
* `/var/lib/uping` holds the local CA (`root.key`) and the leaves. `apt purge`
  leaves it in place on purpose: deleting the CA would silently untrust every
  client that installed its root. Remove the directory by hand if you really
  mean to.
* A removed package stops the service; the unit is not re-enabled on a later
  install unless you enable it again.
